/*!
    \page qtros2-communication-patterns.html
    \title Choosing a Communication Pattern
    \ingroup qtros2-guides

    \keyword topic
    \keyword service
    \keyword action
    \keyword parameter
    \keyword publish subscribe
    \keyword request response
    \keyword long-running task
    \keyword communication pattern
    \keyword when to use

    \brief When to use topics, services, actions, or parameters, and the QML
    types the bridge provides for each.

    ROS 2 gives nodes four ways to exchange information, and the Qt ROS2 Bridge
    exposes all four directly in QML. Picking the right one is almost always a
    question about the \e shape of the interaction — how many participants there
    are, whether the caller expects an answer, and how long the work takes — not
    about the data itself. This guide describes each pattern, when it fits, and
    the QML types that implement it.

    \section1 At a glance

    \table
    \header
        \li Pattern
        \li Direction
        \li Returns a result?
        \li Best for
    \row
        \li \b Topic
        \li Many-to-many, one-way stream
        \li No
        \li Continuous or event-driven data with no reply: sensor readings,
            poses, velocity commands, status.
    \row
        \li \b Service
        \li One-to-one request/response
        \li Yes, once, quickly
        \li A short call that must return an answer: a query, or a command that
            reports whether it succeeded.
    \row
        \li \b Action
        \li One-to-one goal with feedback
        \li Yes, eventually, with progress
        \li A long-running task you want to monitor and be able to cancel:
            navigation, motion, a scripted routine.
    \row
        \li \b Parameter
        \li Named, typed configuration value
        \li n/a (read/write state)
        \li Tuning knobs and configuration that change rarely and belong to a
            node, not to a message stream.
    \endtable

    A useful rule of thumb: if the sender does not care who is listening and
    never waits for a reply, use a \b topic. If it needs an answer and the work
    is quick, use a \b service. If the work takes long enough that you want
    progress updates or a cancel button, use an \b action. If you are describing
    \e how a node should behave rather than sending it data, use a \b parameter.

    \section1 Topics: one-way streams

    A topic is an anonymous, many-to-many channel. Any number of publishers can
    write to it and any number of subscribers can read from it; neither side
    knows the other exists, and a publisher never blocks or waits for
    acknowledgement. This makes topics the right choice for data that flows
    continuously or fires on events — laser scans, camera frames, odometry,
    joint states, velocity commands — and for anything where "fire and forget"
    is acceptable.

    In QML each message type has a generated \c{<Msg>Publisher} and
    \c{<Msg>Subscriber}, declared as children of a \l{Qt ROS2 Core QML
    Types}{Node}:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.GeometryMsgs

    Node {
        nodeName: "example_node"

        PoseStampedPublisher {
            id: posePublisher
            topic: "/pose"
        }

        PoseStampedSubscriber {
            topic: "/other_pose"
            onMessageReceived: console.log(message.pose.position.x)
        }
    }
    \endqml

    Topics carry no delivery guarantee by default; you tune reliability,
    history, and durability through \l{Qt ROS2 Core QML Types}{QualityOfService}
    on the entity's \c qos property. Because there is no reply, a subscriber
    that misses a message simply never sees it — if you need to know that the
    other side acted, you want a service or an action instead.

    \b{See:} \l{Simple Publisher}, \l{Simple Subscriber}.

    \section1 Services: request and response

    A service is a one-to-one call: the client sends a request and receives
    exactly one response. Use it when the caller genuinely needs the answer and
    the work is quick and bounded — a query ("what is the current map?"), or a
    command whose outcome matters ("switch the lamp on; did it work?"). Services
    have no separate error channel: failure is reported \e in-band, as ordinary
    fields of the response value.

    Both ends are declared as children of a \c Node. A client can call by
    binding its \c request property or imperatively with \c callService(), which
    returns a JavaScript promise:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.StdSrvs

    SetBoolServiceClient {
        id: lampClient
        topic: "/lamp"
    }
    // ...
    lampClient.callService(true).then(response => {
        console.log(response.success, response.message)
    })
    \endqml

    A server answers each request, either from a declarative \c response binding
    or from a \c handler callback. It never blocks the GUI thread, and it can
    defer a reply and send it later.

    A service is the wrong tool if the work can take a long time: the caller is
    left waiting with no progress and no way to cancel, and a slow server ties
    up the call. For that, reach for an action.

    \b{See:} \l{Simple Service}, \l{Simple Service Client},
    \l{Qt ROS2 std_srvs QML Types}.

    \section1 Actions: long-running goals

    An action is built on top of topics and services to manage a task that takes
    time. The client sends a \e goal; the server accepts or rejects it, streams
    \e feedback while it works, and finally reports a \e result. The goal can be
    canceled, and its status is observable throughout. Use an action whenever a
    request may take seconds or longer and you want a progress indicator, a
    cancel button, or both — navigation goals, arm motions, scripted sequences.

    The generated \c{<Action>ActionClient} exposes \c sendGoal() (returning a
    promise), a \c feedback property, an \c ActionState, and \c cancelGoal():

    \qml
    navClient.sendGoal({ x: 2.0, y: 1.0 })   // goal message fields
    // navClient.feedback updates as the server reports progress
    // navClient.state reflects Accepted, Succeeded, Canceled, Aborted, ...
    \endqml

    The generated \c{<Action>ActionServer} emits \c goalReceived with a goal
    handle whose methods — \c publishFeedback(), \c succeed(), \c abort(),
    \c canceled() — drive the goal to completion and whose \c cancelRequested
    property lets long work bail out cleanly.

    Reserve actions for genuinely long or cancelable work. For a call that
    returns immediately, the extra goal/feedback/result machinery is overhead a
    service avoids.

    \b{See:} \l{TurtleSim Controller}.

    \section1 Parameters: node configuration

    Parameters are named, typed values that configure a node — thresholds,
    frame names, gains, feature switches. They are not a data channel: they
    change rarely, they belong to a specific node, and they persist as that
    node's state rather than flowing as messages. Reach for a parameter when
    you are describing \e how a node should behave, and for a topic, service, or
    action when you are moving data or commands.

    The bridge covers both sides of the parameter system:

    \list
        \li \l{Qt ROS2 Core QML Types}{Parameter} declares and owns a parameter
            on the \e local node, with a type, description, optional read-only
            flag, and range constraints. It reports external changes so the UI
            can follow them.
        \li \l{Qt ROS2 Core QML Types}{RemoteParameter} gets and sets a
            parameter on \e another node by name, tracking the remote value
            live — the natural way to build a settings panel for a driver or
            controller running elsewhere on the graph.
    \endlist

    \qml
    import QtRos2.Core

    RemoteParameter {
        remoteNode: "/camera_driver"
        name: "exposure"
        // value is two-way: reading reflects the remote node,
        // writing pushes the new value to it
    }
    \endqml

    \section1 Combining patterns

    Real applications use several patterns at once, and a single logical feature
    often spans more than one. A navigation panel might set a planner's tuning
    through \b parameters, dispatch a route as an \b action so it can show
    progress and offer a cancel button, publish manual overrides on a \c cmd_vel
    \b topic, and query the current map through a \b service. Choose each channel
    independently, by the shape of that specific interaction.

    Two cross-cutting concepts apply regardless of the pattern you pick:

    \list
        \li \l{Qt ROS2 Core QML Types}{QualityOfService} governs delivery
            semantics for topics (and the underlying transport of services and
            actions) — reliability, history depth, and durability.
        \li \l{Qt ROS2 Transforms QML Types}{transforms} (TF) carry the spatial
            relationships between coordinate frames, which most of the data on
            your topics is expressed in.
    \endlist

    \section1 Where to go next

    \list
        \li Work through the \l{Qt ROS2 Examples}, which introduce the patterns
            in roughly this order.
        \li For picking the right message type once you have chosen a topic, see
            \l{Choosing Sensor Message Types}.
        \li For sending velocity commands and navigation goals specifically, see
            \l{Teleoperation and Remote Control}.
        \li For the full list of generated types, see the
            \l{Qt ROS2 QML Types} reference.
    \endlist
*/
